            <div xml:id="d992">

                <pb facs="0756" n="640" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg640"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 703.3212/2.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Brazilian Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brazilian Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-04-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 25, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: I have the honor to
                    acknowledge the receipt of your excellency’s note of the 24th instant, and beg
                    to thank you very much for the arrangements which have been made with the
                    British Ambassador to turn over to our Minister in Mexico the archives and
                    records of the American Embassy.</p>
                <p>Assuring you that we lost no time in transmitting your telegram to our Minister
                    there, in our cipher, I request your excellency to accept the renewed assurances
                    of my highest consideration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">D. da Gama</hi></signed>.</closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Note.</hi>—On April 25, 1 p.m., Mr.
                        O’Shaughnessy telegraphed the note from the Foreign Office breaking off
                        diplomatic relations and his reply thereto. This telegram is printed in its
                        chronological order under Political Affairs, <ref target="#pg490">page
                            490</ref>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
